## Solution

Let $G = B_n(\mathbb{C})$ be the group of invertible upper-triangular $n\times n$ matrices, acting on $V = \mathbb{C}^n$ (column vectors) by left multiplication. Let $e_1,\dots,e_n$ be the standard basis and define
$$V_k := \operatorname{span}(e_1,\dots,e_k), \quad k = 0,1,\dots,n,$$
with $V_0 = 0$ and $V_n = V$.

**Claim.** The $\mathbb{C}G$-submodules of $V$ are exactly $V_0, V_1, \dots, V_n$.

---

### Step 1. Each $V_k$ is a $G$-submodule.

For $g = (g_{ij}) \in G$ (so $g_{ij}=0$ for $i>j$) and any basis vector $e_j$,
$$g\, e_j = \sum_{i=1}^{j} g_{ij}\, e_i \in \operatorname{span}(e_1,\dots,e_j) \subseteq V_k \quad\text{whenever } j \le k.$$
Hence $g(V_k) \subseteq V_k$ for every $g\in G$, so each $V_k$ is $G$-invariant.

---

### Step 2. Any $G$-submodule is a direct sum of coordinate lines.

The diagonal subgroup $T = \{ \operatorname{diag}(t_1,\dots,t_n) : t_i \in \mathbb{C}^* \}$ is contained in $G$. As a $T$-module,
$$V = \bigoplus_{i=1}^{n} \mathbb{C}\,e_i,$$
where $T$ acts on $\mathbb{C}e_i$ via the character $\chi_i : \operatorname{diag}(t_1,\dots,t_n) \mapsto t_i$. The characters $\chi_1,\dots,\chi_n$ are pairwise distinct (they are the coordinate projections on $(\mathbb{C}^*)^n$). By the linear independence of distinct characters (equivalently, the weight-space decomposition for a torus action), every $T$-submodule of $V$ is a direct sum of weight spaces. Since every $G$-submodule is in particular a $T$-submodule, any $G$-submodule $W$ has the form
$$W = \operatorname{span}(e_i : i \in S)$$
for some subset $S \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}$.

---

### Step 3. $G$-invariance forces $S$ to be an initial segment.

The elementary unipotent elements $u_{ij}(c) := I + c\,E_{ij} \in G$ (for $i < j$ and $c \in \mathbb{C}$, where $E_{ij}$ is the matrix unit) act on basis vectors by
$$u_{ij}(c)\, e_k = \begin{cases} e_k & k \neq j, \\ e_j + c\,e_i & k = j. \end{cases}$$

Suppose $j \in S$. Then $e_j \in W$, and $G$-invariance gives $u_{ij}(c)\,e_j = e_j + c\,e_i \in W$ for all $c \in \mathbb{C}$ and all $i < j$. Taking $c = 1$ (and using $e_j \in W$) yields $e_i \in W$, i.e. $i \in S$. Therefore
$$j \in S,\; i < j \;\Longrightarrow\; i \in S.$$
This downward-closure means $S$ is an initial segment: $S = \{1, 2, \dots, k\}$ for some $k \in \{0,\dots,n\}$, so $W = V_k$.

---

### Conclusion

The $\mathbb{C}G$-submodules of $V$ are precisely
$$\boxed{\,V_k = \operatorname{span}(e_1,\dots,e_k), \quad k = 0, 1, \dots, n\,}.$$
They form a single chain $0 = V_0 \subset V_1 \subset \cdots \subset V_n = V$, so $V$ is a **uniserial** $\mathbb{C}G$-module.

